Dear INLA expert group,
I am quite new to the INLA approach and I've recently started to look at the R-INLA package for an analysis on which I would appreciate your advice and suggestions.

I want to understand if the distribution of a fishing fleet in a certain time period is associated to particular hydrographic conditions which are known to affect the distribution of fish.

I have:
_ 1-hour time interval GPS locations (PING) that describe the position of each active vessel in the fleet
_ high resolution raster with hydrographic information (EnvVar) on the whole area
_ high resolution raster with depth information (Depth)

I think the problem can be approached with a Gaussian log-Cox model as implemented in R-INLA.
To do so, I've followed the example in the tutorial by Krainski and Lindgren (2013) chapter 8, page 72-74.

Points where I would like a comment/suggestions are:

_ I have associated a value of EnvVar and Depth to each fleet PING. Because in the tutorial "0" is assigned to the triangle vertices, I suppose I should associate a value for EnvVar and Depth to these vertices, correct?
_ modifying the example in the tutorial I've specified the model as follows:

mod1 <- inla(y ~ 0 + f(EnvVar, model="rw2") + f(Depth, model="rw2") + f(i, model=spde),
             family='poisson', data=inla.stack.data(stk.pp), control.predictor=list(A=inla.stack.A(stk.pp)), E=inla.stack.data(stk.pp)$e)

Is this a correct formulation to look for the effect of EnvVar and Depth on the distribution of the fishing fleet?

Thank you in advance for any help

Best regards
Valerio

