From ue7a8779ef7a944e5 at groups.google.com  Fri Feb 28 01:57:31 2014
From: ue7a8779ef7a944e5 at groups.google.com (James Thorson)
Date: Fri, 28 Feb 2014 01:57:31 +0000
Subject: Geometric anisotropy
Message-ID: <N4NtzDlZu_w.d7cqPYAJ7P8J@r-inla-discussion-group.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/r-inla-discussion-group/c/N4NtzDlZu_w/m/d7cqPYAJ7P8J>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Hi all,

I'm writing to inquire whether there is documentation for how to implement "geometric anisotropy" in R-INLA and/or the SPDE approach?  Geometric anisotropy is obviously a very frequent model to explore when isotropy fails to explain the data well and, while I see some discussion of general anisotropy and nonstationarity, it would be convenient to have this intermediate level to explore.

Cheers,
Jim

